= Homeostasis — Technical =

== Variables and loops ==
A homeostatic model specifies a controlled quantity x, a reference or admissible range, measured state, disturbances, actuator or effector responses and system dynamics. A basic negative-feedback controller responds to a deviation in a way that can reduce that deviation. Its stability depends on delays, gain, plant dynamics and coupling to other variables; negative-feedback architecture alone does not guarantee stability.

== Biological mechanisms ==
The controlled quantity can be represented by a concentration, temperature, osmotic state or other physiological variable. Membrane transport, metabolism, neural and hormonal signaling, and behavioral responses may act together. In an open system, near-constant x can coexist with continuous fluxes of mass and energy: a nonequilibrium steady state need not be chemical or thermodynamic equilibrium.

== Testing a homeostatic claim ==
Evidence may include a controlled perturbation, a measured compensatory response, an identified effector and a dynamic model that predicts recovery under stated conditions. A model should identify failure limits and distinguish baseline adaptation or feedforward control from feedback correction.

